  "enhanced_information": "T regulatory cells (Tregs) are a subset of CD4+ T cells that play a crucial role in maintaining immunologic tolerance. They develop primarily in the thymus and also in peripheral lymphoid organs, expressing CD25 and relying on forkhead box P3 (Foxp3) for their regulatory functions. Tregs inhibit the activation of potentially harmful lymphocytes that could react against self-antigens. A key function of Tregs is the secretion of anti-inflammatory cytokines, particularly Interleukin-10 (IL-10), which is known for its immunosuppressive properties and role in promoting tolerance. Therefore, the correct answer is Interleukin-10."
